  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nMid level shortwave ridge is shifting east into the Midwest as weak \ntroughing across the plains returns southerly flow to the area. \nHighs today are near seasonal norms in the low to middle 50s as mid-\nhigh clouds increase from the west this evening. While winds remain \ngenerally light overnight, residual cloud cover will hold overnight \nlows Saturday morning to the low and middle 30s. \n\nThe lee trough deepens on Saturday over the High Plains ahead of an \napproaching shortwave trough. Southerly WAA pushes highs into the \n50s once again as the cold front enters north central Kansas in the \nmid afternoon. Decent mid level frontogenesis on the northwest flank \nof the wave along with timing and forcing consistency the last few \ndays among deterministic and ensemble guidance will result in \nwidespread rain showers Saturday evening into early Sunday morning \n(80-100% probability).  The latest runs from the HRRR and RRFS show \na strong consensus in rain being the dominant precipitation type. \nStrong post frontal CAA follows behind the front overnight, quickly \ncooling vertical thermal profiles below freezing. Despite one or two \ndeterministic models attempting to mix light snow with the rain \n(along and north of the highway 36 corridor) early Sunday morning, \nREFS and NBM probabilistic snow guidance are less than 10%, \nindicating an all-rain event is the most likely outcome. Total \nrainfall amounts are generally less than a half of an inch.\n\nGusty winds are also of note behind the front late Saturday night \nthrough Sunday afternoon from the northwest at 15 to 25 mph \nsustained. Highest gusts up to 50 mph are possible between midnight \nand sunrise Sunday as the sfc low tightens up the pressure gradient \nbehind the front. The colder air moving in will return highs Sunday \nback into the 40s, becoming the upper 20s for lows Monday morning.  \n\nEnsemble guidance features a good consensus on westerly flow setting \nup over the central conus through the first half of next week. While \na low amplitude shortwave trough traverses the plains Monday \nevening, high pressure influence over the Gulf should suppress any \nmoisture for precipitation in northeast Kansas. Weak southerly \nreturn flow yields minimal temperature spread among the NBM, \nrebounding highs to the 60s with overnight lows in the 30s. \nConfidence remains low in the next southern stream upper low's \ntiming and track by Thursday as it ejects into the plains. NBM pops \nare up to likely during this period, which may be a bit aggressive \ngiven the wide variance in GEFS/ENS solutions.",
